{
	title: 'Colours as code',
	description: 'src/common/colors.ts is the theme: one exported object whose top-level fields are either plain values (fgL: 0.85, fgC: 0.02, ...) or functions computing a style from them at call time (assistant: () => ({ fg: [colors.fgL, colors.fgC, 55] })). local.ts overrides a top-level field (colors.fgL = 0.9) and everything derived follows. No string references like $fgL and no colors.ason. The terminal turns styles into escape codes; the host generates CSS from the same object for the web page, which never sees the functions. A light/dark choice, if any, is a setting. Reference for the palette: the old colors.ason and colors.ts (artifacts); keep its look, not its format.',
	status: 'planned',
	needs: ['cc']
}
